  "worked_solution": "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature: \n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(3)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10}),\\,-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{25}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10}),\\,0⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{2}{25}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{25}⟨1,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{25}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
